        {
            "number": 5,
            "revision": "2b9ed05cb67c9c8eb4d2279898f202a1119e8754",
            "parents": [
                "3a853d8eaaaf5196389b4da60652c503d5f3f3d5"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/37/88537/5",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "createdOn": 1779991714,
            "author": {
                "name": "Ed Tanous",
                "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                "username": "edtanous"
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "src/report.cpp",
                    "line": 59,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "If anything else in Report::Report() throws an exception, then Report::~Report() wont be called and we wont call `objServer->remove_interface(deleteIface);` so there is a potential leak. Same goes with all calls to add_interface(). So probably wrap the constructor in a try/catch/re-throw with a `if (deleteIface) { objServer->remove_interface(deleteIface); }`\n\nAlso, this looks like a generalized problem/pattern we need to solve with add_interface()?\n\nThat being said, if any of these constructors throw, main() will exit so we dont have to worry about memory leaks. So, probably sets the tone of the comment to a (nit)."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/report.cpp",
                    "line": 59,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> If anything else in Report::Report() throws an exception, then Report::~Report() wont be called and we wont call `objServer->remove_interface(deleteIface);` so there is a potential leak.\n\nThere's no leak.  Object is still owned by the object_server and will be destroyed when it is destroyed, but point taken that we might have an object longer than we'd prefer.\n\n> Same goes with all calls to add_interface(). So probably wrap the constructor in a try/catch/re-throw with a `if (deleteIface) { objServer->remove_interface(deleteIface); }`\n\nHuh?  This would be the only place we do that.  If this is something we want to hold on, I'd probably rather move all the add_interface calls into an init() method of some kind and out of the constructor.\n\n> \n> Also, this looks like a generalized problem/pattern we need to solve with add_interface()?\n\nYes, that's exactly what I'm trying to do, but having this callback method is somewhat getting in the way.\n\n> \n> That being said, if any of these constructors throw, main() will exit so we dont have to worry about memory leaks. So, probably sets the tone of the comment to a (nit).\n\nI would tend to agree.  In terms of code cleanliness for this patch, I don't think catching exceptions thrown here make sense to propagate, especially when they just throw to main and don't cause a leak.\n\nI agree, the pattern could be improved."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/report.cpp",
                    "line": 59,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Let me know what, in the context of this commit you think we should do for the above.  I really don't want to add a bunch of boilerplate catching in this, and this is a pattern we do (right or wrong) in a bunch of asio daemons."
                },
                {
                    "file": "src/report.cpp",
                    "line": 59,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Amithash Prasad",
                        "email": "amithash@meta.com",
                        "username": "amithash"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                }
            ],
